1
00:00:17,523 --> 00:00:24,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we've got a question on the two types of extreme mentioned in the suttas.

2
00:00:24,706 --> 00:00:34,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the two types of extreme can be clarified in what the middle way is.

3
00:00:36,411 --> 00:00:41,673
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems that the middle way cannot be the way of moderation, because moderation still takes part in both extremes.

4
00:00:42,273 --> 00:00:46,175
[SPEAKER_00]: If one is loving or hating, the middle way cannot be a little bit of both.

5
00:00:47,617 --> 00:00:54,819
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would it be correct to say that the middle way is the opposite way of whatever our present craving is?

6
00:00:56,019 --> 00:01:04,922
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, when there is love, one should develop non-love, and when there is hate, one should develop non-hate.

7
00:01:04,982 --> 00:01:08,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in Dhammacakasutta, the Buddha says these two domains

8
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:20,281
[SPEAKER_00]: The two extremes, the sensual indulgence and the self-mortification,

9
00:01:45,669 --> 00:02:08,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually, commonly, they're sort of watered down in a sense that they're taken to mean like this kind of obvious overdoing of indulging of sensual pleasures and like a obvious self-evident like a self-torture or something like that.

10
00:02:08,604 --> 00:02:12,587
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's actually much more subtle than that in a sense that

11
00:02:13,735 --> 00:02:24,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, the extreme indulgence in sensuality partakes in the domain of sensual pleasures, but also that domain can be very very subtle.

12
00:02:24,801 --> 00:02:36,168
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you engage in some subtle ways with sensuality, it's still within the same domain of sensuality, which means middle way does not apply to it.

13
00:02:36,628 --> 00:02:37,549
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense,

14
00:02:44,613 --> 00:02:50,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, there is a spectrum, from subtle to coarse, and it doesn't matter what

15
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:19,304
[SPEAKER_00]: At what place in that spectrum you are, if it's towards sensuality, it's still the domain of central indulgence.

16
00:03:19,744 --> 00:03:26,549
[SPEAKER_00]: If that spectrum is towards the self-mortification, it's still within the domain of self-mortification.

17
00:03:26,609 --> 00:03:29,271
[SPEAKER_00]: So, middle way is neither.

18
00:03:29,611 --> 00:03:31,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't apply to any of those.

19
00:03:31,633 --> 00:03:40,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why this needs clarifying is because in sort of in contemporary notions of Buddhism, as I said, things just naturally are watered down.

20
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:51,621
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what this question points out.

21
00:03:51,641 --> 00:04:04,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if it were only moderation, means, okay, so you could still have love towards sensuality, or hate towards pain, and so on, and then you just need to sort of be moderate in regard to two.

22
00:04:04,590 --> 00:04:07,872
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, middle way is the the subtlest thing to see.

23
00:04:08,673 --> 00:04:17,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not something you can arrive at through mere moderate practice, because if if that were possible, we wouldn't need the Buddha, you know,

24
00:04:18,222 --> 00:04:21,786
[SPEAKER_00]: It shapes the universe when when he discovers the middle way and so on.

25
00:04:22,226 --> 00:04:26,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be something every other person would sort of stumble upon.

26
00:04:26,210 --> 00:04:36,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Middle way can only be discovered on the level of, as it says here, the opposite way of wherever your present craving is directing.

27
00:04:37,361 --> 00:04:41,405
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your present craving is towards self-mortification and denial,

28
00:04:42,325 --> 00:04:46,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the middle way is non-craving towards self-mortification and denial.

29
00:04:47,507 --> 00:04:54,830
[SPEAKER_00]: If your present way is craving towards sensuality and sense gratification, the middle way is non-desire towards sensuality.

30
00:04:54,950 --> 00:05:03,213
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's not... it cannot be adhered to as a set of behavior or method, or do this and you follow the middle way.

31
00:05:03,793 --> 00:05:05,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as we often say,

32
00:05:06,994 --> 00:05:12,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Where the middle way will be found, depends on where your attention is currently aiming at.

33
00:05:12,538 --> 00:05:13,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intention, sorry.

34
00:05:14,379 --> 00:05:19,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why knowing your intentions means knowing where things are rooted in, in your mind.

35
00:05:19,823 --> 00:05:23,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this rooted in desire, or real will, or denial, or any of the hindrance?

36
00:05:23,886 --> 00:05:27,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the middle way would be the absence of those unbeneficial states.

37
00:05:27,749 --> 00:05:29,450
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you need to be sensory-strained, sure.

38
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:30,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't be spilling out.

39
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:34,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to know the intentions behind your actions.

40
00:05:34,831 --> 00:05:38,132
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are aiming at the...

41
00:05:39,452 --> 00:05:43,113
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, some practice, ascetic practice.

42
00:05:43,153 --> 00:05:49,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, you got inspired and you want to be staying awake all night and meditating every day.

43
00:05:50,175 --> 00:05:53,236
[SPEAKER_00]: That in itself, it's neither good nor bad.

44
00:05:53,916 --> 00:05:57,017
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your motivation behind wanting to do that.

45
00:05:58,040 --> 00:06:03,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reasons for you wanting to do such practice, that's what you would see, if this is wholesome or unwholesome.

46
00:06:04,465 --> 00:06:16,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And that needs to be seen, because otherwise, you're just... the entire project, the entire inspiring project, is rooted in an unwholesome intention.

47
00:06:16,394 --> 00:06:22,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the roots are not wholesome, they're rooted in probably a degree of ill-will, denial, aversion,

48
00:06:23,378 --> 00:06:25,159
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's taking you in that direction.

49
00:06:25,199 --> 00:06:36,406
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would never... didn't want to prescribe the ascetic practices as mandatory practices, because they don't always result in uprooting of passion.

50
00:06:37,067 --> 00:06:41,609
[SPEAKER_00]: In themselves, by themselves, they do not guarantee uprooting of passion and ill-will.

51
00:06:42,130 --> 00:06:42,730
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary,

52
00:06:43,376 --> 00:06:57,514
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were rooted in wrong intentions, in intentions of conceit, intentions of aversion, intentions of restlessness and impatience, then they will increase your passion and your aversion and your conceit and your impatience.

53
00:06:58,455 --> 00:06:59,476
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind will grow if

54
00:07:00,912 --> 00:07:06,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Things rooted in unwholesome intentions, when they are rooted in unwholesome intentions, are not pursued, not welcomed.

55
00:07:07,554 --> 00:07:16,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, eh... certain things that are kind of neutral in themselves, can sometimes be rooted in unwholesome intentions, sometimes in wholesome intentions.

56
00:07:16,317 --> 00:07:19,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not that you must never do them, or that you can always do them.

57
00:07:20,358 --> 00:07:23,779
[SPEAKER_00]: So, things you should never do, are things that are against the precepts.

58
00:07:23,799 --> 00:07:24,519
[SPEAKER_00]: That's very clear.

59
00:07:24,739 --> 00:07:25,700
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't require any

60
00:07:26,944 --> 00:07:36,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Great philosophical pondering and wisdom just requires a bit of faith and taking on those behaviors as a mode of being that you train within.

61
00:07:36,887 --> 00:07:44,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, within the precepts, there will be things that are, as I said, sometimes, you know, beneficial, sometimes not.

62
00:07:44,489 --> 00:07:52,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Externally, they look the same, those things, but internally, they are completely different things because they're rooted in different type of intentions.

63
00:07:52,312 --> 00:07:56,113
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's paramount, really,

64
00:07:58,072 --> 00:08:19,349
[SPEAKER_00]: And in the precepts, I need to pretty much refrain an impulse that, you know, most people have to kind of overly clarify the set of behavior and methods and meditations or set of determinations in ascetic practices and instead

65
00:08:20,382 --> 00:08:29,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the precepts, stay on the level of that kind of ambiguity of my own intention, and sometimes like this, sometimes like that, because that's where the practice is.

66
00:08:30,105 --> 00:08:32,806
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can ask yourself, which is harder?

67
00:08:33,206 --> 00:08:35,707
[SPEAKER_00]: To go along with centrality?

68
00:08:36,968 --> 00:08:41,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Or to build up a determination and just say no to everything?

69
00:08:42,029 --> 00:08:47,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it harder to stay on the level of doubting mind,

70
00:08:47,850 --> 00:08:55,313
[SPEAKER_00]: that tries to pressure you to act constantly, that refuses to listen to the reason.

71
00:08:55,813 --> 00:09:16,901
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is it harder to stay on that level and endure the mind pressuring you, than to just overly determine and dismiss that whole domain and just commit to a blind practice of, I'll never sleep at night, or I will walk six hours a day doing walking meditation, or I will never speak, or I will never do this, or then go the other extreme and just do, you know, everything completely unrestrained.

72
00:09:17,607 --> 00:09:19,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's always that similar of the deer herd and the bait.

73
00:09:20,568 --> 00:09:29,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, eh... you can eat the bait carelessly or you can completely say no to all types of food because it might contain the bait.

74
00:09:29,853 --> 00:09:36,276
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you'll starve or you will learn how to discern where the bait is and then just don't eat that.

75
00:09:36,376 --> 00:09:38,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why... that's why the middle way is the hardest way.

76
00:09:39,781 --> 00:09:43,583
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the other way is the ignoble way, because it will not bring understanding.

77
00:09:43,663 --> 00:09:56,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, externally, you know, some severe practices of self-mortification are far more, like, inspiring to the eye, like the commitment, the determination, the persistence, and so on, the endurance.

78
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:06,334
[SPEAKER_00]: But internally, if they're completely rooted in your desire, conceit, and power of will that refuses to, basically, endure things on the level of

79
00:10:07,034 --> 00:10:11,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind won't... like, mind intending to do things out of desire, and you're not giving in to that.

80
00:10:11,696 --> 00:10:17,119
[SPEAKER_00]: It's easier to just cover up the desire and commit to a set of blind practice that won't make you think about it.

81
00:10:17,179 --> 00:10:18,780
[SPEAKER_00]: But that... that's the point.

82
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:19,801
[SPEAKER_00]: It will have to run out.

83
00:10:20,601 --> 00:10:29,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Sooner or later, that will have to run out, as the simile of the deer says, and then you go back eating all the food carelessly, because you don't... you haven't learned where the bait is.

84
00:10:31,347 --> 00:10:33,628
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowing the intentions behind your actions,

85
00:10:34,045 --> 00:10:52,315
[SPEAKER_00]: behind the actions of the body, the speech, the mind, throughout the day, on the basis of impeccable precepts, that will result in, like, sustained recollectedness, mindfulness, it will result in composure, and it will result in, as I often say, enduring things on the right level.

86
00:10:53,592 --> 00:11:12,344
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if through that framework, you see that increasing the certain ascetic aspects of your life, introducing some asceticism into your life, will actually bring this further to fulfillment, this context, this understanding, this clarity.

87
00:11:12,845 --> 00:11:15,847
[SPEAKER_00]: But then it will be, again, it will be guided by the right perspective.

88
00:11:16,507 --> 00:11:21,130
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're just doing it because it kind of makes you feel good, makes you feel righteous, makes you feel inspired,

89
00:11:24,271 --> 00:11:36,458
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're a domain of sensuality and domain of self-mortification and denial, well, the domain of enduring your intentions without acting out of them, is the domain of the middle way.

90
00:11:36,938 --> 00:11:47,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the domain where the hindrances are, where the pressure and pains of desire are, of ill-will, of sloth and laziness, of restlessness, of worry, of doubt.

91
00:11:48,626 --> 00:11:50,947
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... that's why... that's where the practice is.

92
00:11:50,967 --> 00:11:52,488
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to endure things on that level,

93
00:11:53,502 --> 00:12:07,012
[SPEAKER_00]: then your mind will have to grow, even if you never engage in, you know, some extreme ascetic practices, but if you never lose the sight of the domain of your own intention, like of your own choices you're making.

94
00:12:07,172 --> 00:12:11,195
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, you know, I keep saying that, you know,

95
00:12:12,058 --> 00:12:31,986
[SPEAKER_00]: People's minds, generally, and stress that enter your mind, and your life, and it burdens you, and overwhelms you, is not the result of some metaphysical ignorance, nor is the result of you not having practiced focusing, or noting meditation, or watching your cessation long enough.

96
00:12:32,186 --> 00:12:32,326
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

97
00:12:33,647 --> 00:12:41,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Unwholesome states, always, there's only one way that those unwholesome states can enter your mind, and burden your life,

98
00:12:42,037 --> 00:12:52,060
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is intentional choice towards greed or lust, towards ill-will, aversion, and towards distraction and indolence.

99
00:12:52,220 --> 00:12:52,661
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

100
00:12:53,401 --> 00:12:58,423
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not because you slipped a moment, you didn't watch your mind close enough, you weren't concentrated enough.

101
00:12:58,483 --> 00:12:58,683
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

102
00:12:59,123 --> 00:13:11,427
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you still keep choosing consciously, like, eh... in in full awareness, although you might, you know, ignore that, but every choice is something you're conscious of.

103
00:13:12,475 --> 00:13:18,621
[SPEAKER_00]: And the disturbances and unwholesome states can only enter through such choices and persist.

104
00:13:19,302 --> 00:13:29,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to stop making such choices, sustain that state of not making choices rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, and then they will cease to persist.

105
00:13:29,172 --> 00:13:33,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Those unwholesome states will fade away, because you're not adding more fuel to their fire.

106
00:13:34,457 --> 00:13:42,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, so... and then, if sometimes, you know, a person think, oh, but no, I still need to concentrate in order to be able to catch those choices.

107
00:13:42,202 --> 00:13:51,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, choices are not on the level of, like, a momentary flow of your consciousness and so on, that you must kind of... mustn't blink, because you miss your choice.

108
00:13:51,468 --> 00:13:51,608
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

109
00:13:52,812 --> 00:13:53,252
[SPEAKER_00]: That's quite evident.

110
00:13:53,352 --> 00:14:04,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Choice is something you intentionally intend, something you choose to make, something you choose to act out of, something you choose to welcome, accept, delight in, and so on.

111
00:14:05,123 --> 00:14:13,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... it's not something you can be unaware of or it's... or something that can accidentally slip you.

112
00:14:13,210 --> 00:14:19,415
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why, fundamentally, path to nibbāna and enlightenment is always within

113
00:14:20,102 --> 00:14:23,584
[SPEAKER_00]: your reach and within your own control, in that sense.

114
00:14:23,604 --> 00:14:34,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that you can choose nibbāna directly, but you can abstain from choices that have taken you away from that which nibbāna is, that is keeping you away from that which nibbāna is.

115
00:14:34,972 --> 00:14:44,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if those choices are abandoned and then purified, whether you want it or not, your mind is heading towards nibbāna.

116
00:14:45,099 --> 00:14:47,981
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of blanket rules and behaviors, that's the precepts.

117
00:14:48,041 --> 00:14:48,441
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

118
00:14:49,383 --> 00:15:00,930
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, that's just to contain you, so that you can start seeing your intention, intentions towards same things rooted in different intention, different things rooted in the same intention, and so on.

119
00:15:01,090 --> 00:15:10,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you're contained within the precepts, your intentions have potential, basically, to become, to be understood, to be seen even more clearly.

120
00:15:10,615 --> 00:15:13,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Your choices, responsibility for those, and so on.

121
00:15:14,177 --> 00:15:18,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the question is just of not falling into a trap of

122
00:15:19,762 --> 00:15:39,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Or giving in to that kind of path of less resistance, which is the path of further than adopting sets of behaviors, techniques and methods, so that you don't have to think about or endure the states of doubt in your mind, the states of uncertainty.

123
00:15:44,223 --> 00:15:44,904
[SPEAKER_00]: but it was a different thing.

124
00:15:45,324 --> 00:15:48,087
[SPEAKER_00]: All that needs to be endured until the clarity is developed.

125
00:15:48,668 --> 00:15:51,591
[SPEAKER_00]: And you endure that on the basis of not breaking the precepts.

126
00:15:52,192 --> 00:15:53,113
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's unpleasant.

127
00:15:53,313 --> 00:15:54,394
[SPEAKER_00]: That can be a pleasant.

128
00:15:54,534 --> 00:15:58,699
[SPEAKER_00]: And as the Buddha said in the suttas, how unpleasant that will be, well, depends on how much

129
00:15:59,418 --> 00:16:02,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind was dependent on sensuality beforehand.

130
00:16:02,439 --> 00:16:12,123
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how much... like, how many... how often the hindrances would be... would be pressuring you and pulling you depends on, basically, how often you were giving in to them in the past.

131
00:16:12,544 --> 00:16:16,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... you... you can't really know that up until you're free from them.

132
00:16:16,985 --> 00:16:18,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... then it doesn't really matter.

133
00:16:18,826 --> 00:16:22,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is to endure things on that unpleasant level, when it's unpleasant.

134
00:16:22,148 --> 00:16:23,428
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not always going to be unpleasant.

135
00:16:24,228 --> 00:16:27,730
[SPEAKER_00]: And not... not... not give in to that, eh... like...

136
00:16:28,458 --> 00:16:44,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Overly defined now, sets of practices and methods, and sensation watching, and noting, or whatever else, techniques, they will then sort of make it more mechanical, step-by-step practice, so that you don't have to endure the unpleasant states.

137
00:16:45,084 --> 00:16:53,167
[SPEAKER_00]: While at the same time, that entire commitment to that step-by-step mechanical practice, as I call it, is rooted in the intentions that you're ignoring.

138
00:16:53,988 --> 00:16:54,608
[SPEAKER_00]: And so,

139
00:17:02,961 --> 00:17:08,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that would be the practice of the Middle Way.

140
00:17:08,404 --> 00:17:12,627
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, you're not giving into centrality, but you're not overly denying now either.

141
00:17:13,192 --> 00:17:14,013
[SPEAKER_00]: You're enduring things.

142
00:17:14,313 --> 00:17:22,381
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the simile of the deer, the smart, the wisest deer herd learn that they can't say no to all the food, because they'll starve.

143
00:17:23,002 --> 00:17:29,969
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they learn how to endure the pressure, the pull, the enchantment of the bait.

144
00:17:30,850 --> 00:17:34,953
[SPEAKER_00]: And not act towards the bait, so that then they can eat other food that's not the bait.

145
00:17:35,573 --> 00:17:40,636
[SPEAKER_00]: But until you have endured the bait long enough, you won't see what... what's bait, what it isn't.

146
00:17:40,756 --> 00:17:42,137
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's the whole practice.

147
00:17:42,157 --> 00:17:45,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not giving in, but not running away from it either.

148
00:17:45,299 --> 00:17:47,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Not welcoming it, but not denying it.

149
00:17:47,861 --> 00:17:49,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Allowing it to arise, persist and endure it.

